#!/usr/bin/env xetal
## Calling a function of several arguments, three ways
## (docs/several-arguments.md). Every X_eTaL function takes one
## argument: `x f y` is `(f x) y`, so a third argument goes to the
## function that call gives, as an applied group `(...)_ z`.
##
## Run it with ./demos/apply.xtl or "xetal run demos/apply.xtl"; each
## way prints 4321. "xetal expand demos/apply.xtl" shows what the
## macro of the second way wrote.

## a b c d u:f_: the digits a, b, c and d as one number, a the ones.
u:f_ := { a b c d -> a + (10 * b) + (100 * c) + 1000 * d }

# 1. Written out: one applied group per argument after the second.
((1 u:f_ 2)_ 3)_ 4

# 2. A macro of your own writes that chain (userlibs/Apply.xtlm; in
# the live demo, Apply.xtlm among your files): the function on the
# left, the arguments on the right separated by `;`.
"ap:" u_se< "Apply"
(((1) u:f_ (2))_ (3))_ (4)
(((2 - 1) u:f_ (2))_ (3))_ (4)   # any expression is an argument

# 3. The idiom for three or more arguments: one tuple, no nesting.
## u:g_ (a, b, c, d): the same number, its four digits as one tuple.
u:g_ := { (a, b, c, d) -> a + (10 * b) + (100 * c) + 1000 * d }
u:g_ (1, 2, 3, 4)

# Written out, a call can stop early: a partial application, here the
# function still waiting for d (a tuple comes whole and cannot).
## u:f_irstThree d: 321 plus 1000 d.
u:f_irstThree := (1 u:f_ 2)_ 3
u:f_irstThree 4

